      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The Shoulder Pain Disability Index (SPADI) is a recently published but widely used outcome measure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This study included 136 patients with shoulder disorders. SPADI was first translated and back-translated and then subjected to psychometric validation. Participants completed the Spanish versions of the SPADI, general health (SF-12), the Simple Shoulder Test (SST), Disability of Arm, Shoulder, and Hand (DASH) questionnaires and a pain intensity visual analog scale (VAS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The factors explained 62.8 % of the variance, with an internal consistency of α\u2009=\u20090.916 and 0.860, respectively. The confirmatory factor analysis showed a Comparative Fit Index of 0.82 and a Normed Fit Index of 0.80. The Root Mean Square Error of Aproximation was 0.12. The x (2) test for the 2-factor model was significant (x (2)\u2009=\u2009185.41, df\u2009=\u200962, p\u2009<\u20090.01). The test-retest reliability was high, with an item ranging of the interclass correlation coefficient (ICC) from 0.89 to 0.93. The ICC for the total score was 0.91 (95 % CI 0.88 to 0.94). Measurement error by minimal detectable change (MDC)95 was 12.2 %. In the construct validity analysis, strong positive correlations were observed between Spanish Version of the SPADI and DASH (pain: r\u2009=\u20090.80; p\u2009<\u20090.01; disability: r\u2009=\u20090.76; p\u2009<\u20090.01). Moderate positive correlations were observed between Spanish Version of the SPADI and VAS (pain: r\u2009=\u20090.67; p\u2009<\u20090.01; disability: r\u2009=\u20090.65; p\u2009<\u20090.01). Moderate negative correlations were obtained between Spanish Version of the SPADI and SST-Sp (pain: r\u2009=\u2009-0.71; p\u2009<\u20090.01; disability: r\u2009=\u2009-0.75; p\u2009<\u20090.01). However, pain total Spanish Version of the SPADI was only weakly correlated with physical and mental components of SF-12 (both r\u2009=\u20090.40; p\u2009<\u20090.01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This Spanish version of SPADI demonstrated satisfactory psychometric properties in a patient sample in the hospital setting."
        }
      ]},
